(* RoboLang v2.4 grammar, implemented in src/robolang/parser.py.
   RoboLang 2.3 plus the robot's own charge (decision 0048): one kind, "charge", and one live form, "robot_charge". Differences
   from grammar-v2.3.ebnf are marked [2.4]. No new statement and no new reserved word.
   Everything not shown here is as in grammar-v2.3.ebnf (and the grammars below it): a task and a mission are unchanged.
   Normative text, diagnostics and static rules: spec/robolang-v2.4.md *)

version_decl  = "robolang" , NUMBER , ";" ;                (* [2.4] NUMBER written exactly 2.4 *)

statement     = ... | measure_charge ;                      (* [2.4] one more form of measure; it ends with ";" *)
measure_charge = "measure" , NAME , "charge" , "as" , NAME , [ else_block ] , ";" ;
                                                            (* [2.4] NO "of ... using ...": the charge comes from the robot *)

atom          = ... | live_charge_cmp ;                     (* [2.4] a comparison *)
live_charge_cmp = "robot_charge" , NAME , OP , operand ;    (* [2.4] allowed in a wait until, in a mission's until, and in a choose's where *)
OP            = "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=" ;                   (* [2.4] a charge has no == and no != (E320) *)
operand       = NUMBER , "%" | NAME ;                       (* [2.4] a literal in % (the unit is required), or a binding of kind charge *)

(* [2.4] "charge" and "robot_charge" are recognised by their position and are NOT reserved: a program of any version may still
   use them as names. In a file below 2.4 "measure r charge as c;" is E217 and "robot_charge r" is E204.
   [2.4] The kind: measure r charge as c; binds the charge of the robot r in % (0 to 100). r is any robot of the task, so another
   robot's charge may be read too. It compares with <, <=, >, >= against a literal in % ("25 %") or another charge binding, and
   there is NO ==. A charge is not compared with another kind (E319) and a number needs its unit % (E206). Example:
       measure r charge as c;
       if c < 25 % { ... } else { ... }
   [2.4] An unknown charge (the robot has none in the world) or an uncertainty interval that holds the threshold makes the
   comparison unknown, and an unknown if takes no branch: it fails closed. A charge is sent as a value with its unit:
       communicate r to "lead" with c;      (* sends 64 % *)
   [2.4] The live form: robot_charge r reads the charge each time the wait until is evaluated. It is written ONLY in a wait until
   and in a mission's until (E322 elsewhere: in an if, measure the charge and compare the binding). Example:
       wait until robot_charge r >= 80 % timeout 40 min else { fail "the battery is not at 80 percent in time"; }
   A mission's until may be "robot_charge r < 30 %": it is read after each run line. It is NOT a bound by itself: the mission still
   needs a count, a duration or a time of day (E325).
   [2.4] A choose may keep the robot with enough charge, and ONLY this reading is allowed in a where (E330 otherwise):
       choose robot t from (a, b) where robot_charge t >= 50 % else { fail "no robot has 50 percent"; }
   A candidate whose charge is unknown is excluded. The charge of a candidate is a world fact reported by the fleet manager.
   [2.4] The charge changes ONLY by the scheduled events of the world: there is no drain and no charging model. "do r charge
   charger" is a request to the robot's controller and changes no reading. A request to charge until a threshold is a charge
   action followed by a wait until robot_charge r >= T with the timeout the request states: wait for the event. Only wait for
   advances time in a world with no positions.
   [2.4] The robot's own low-battery protection ALWAYS takes precedence. RoboLang asks; it never overrides the controller, and a
   charge check in a task is never the protection.
   [2.4] A world's declared channels limit what the robot RECEIVES (message_received), never what communicate may send: communicate
   may send on any channel name the request gives.
   [2.4] What 2.4 cannot say (decision 0048 S10; erratum E5 of docs/evidence/bench-errata-1-8.md states why this belongs in the
   grammar file). Decline each as inexpressible:
   - self-tests and their results, faults, requests for service: "run your self-test and tell the lead the result", "tell the lead
     which faults you have". Health beyond robot_ready and sensor_ok is not a charge (G-HEALTH);
   - time until empty and energy for a route: "how many minutes can you still run", "if you will not make it back to the dock".
     A charge cannot be predicted: there is no drain model, and the request gives no threshold (G-PREDICT);
   - arithmetic on a charge (G-CALC);
   - a trigger on the charge: "the moment your battery drops below 20 percent, even in the middle of a round". A mission starts a
     run only when the last one is done, and a check at the start of each run is a different request (G-TRIGGER).
   [2.4] Do not replace a charge check: a request "if your battery is low", "at least 60 percent charge" or "until your battery is
   at least 80 percent" is never robot_ready, never the check dropped, never a wait for of a guessed time, and never another
   robot's charge. Write the charge with the robot, operator and threshold the request states, or decline. Do not invent a
   threshold, an operator, a bound or a stop rule the request does not state.
   [2.4] A request that needs a charge the world does not give (the robot has no charge in the world, or no scheduled event brings it
   to the threshold before the timeout) and that names no fallback is infeasible, not a program. *)

(* Reserved words in a 2.4 file: those of grammar-v2.3.ebnf. "charge" and "robot_charge" are not reserved. *)
